text:
Lina: Customer escalation: intermittent duplicate POSTs when using our TypeScript/Node SDK behind a corporate HTTP proxy. They say when a request times out we abort and retry, but the first request sometimes still arrives at the backend (duplicate side-effects). Attached logs show two POSTs ~200ms apart. Repro steps below.
Raj: Which SDK + undici versions? Node runtime? Is the customer using the SDK's built-in retries or custom retry logic?
Lina: SDK @ 3.2.1, undici 4.17.0 (they're using the global fetch shim), Node 18.16.0. They're using custom retry with AbortController reused across attempts. Proxy: company uses http(s)-proxy-agent on each request.
Matt: Reusing the same AbortController across retries can be risky — once aborted the signal is permanently aborted. But this sounds like the first request wasn't fully cancelled on the socket level before the retry started. Proxies/agents sometimes don't forward the abort to the socket.
Lina: Minimal repro the customer sent (they said this is very close to their code):

```
const controller = new AbortController();

async function attemptFetch(url) {
  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(url, { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({x:1}), signal: controller.signal });
      return await res.json();
    } catch (err) {
      if (i === 2) throw err;
      // wait with exponential backoff
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 100 * (2 ** i)));
      // NOTE: controller is reused here
    }
  }
}
```

They say sometimes the server sees two POST bodies.
Raj: That code is the red flag — controller reuse + proxy. Also undici v4 had some signal handoff differences vs v5/Node built-in fetch. Two immediate asks: can you get a pcap/socket dump? and can we reproduce locally with the same proxy stack?
Sam: Quick try: change to "new AbortController()" inside the loop (one controller per attempt), or don't pass the signal for the retry attempt. Also try undici@5.x or running with node's native fetch (node 20) to see differences.
Lina: I asked the customer to try per-attempt controller and to run with undici 5.x. They ran the per-attempt controller and initial results look promising: no duplicates in 50 attempts. Customer provided a gist with full logs: https://gist.github.com/customer/abcd1234
Raj: I ran a local repro using a proxy-agent (http-proxy-agent) + undici@4.17 + Node 18 and I can reproduce ~15% of the time. With undici@5.1 and creating a fresh AbortController per attempt the issue disappears in my repro runs.
Matt: Preliminary theory: when a proxy-agent attaches to the request it creates a tunnel/socket lifecycle that doesn't immediately propagate the AbortSignal to the underlying request stream in undici@4. That can let the request body finish sending while the app thinks it aborted. When we then trigger a retry the second request goes out — backend sees duplicates. Also body stream cloning/consumption is a factor for non-idempotent requests.
Lina: Customer asked if there's a recommended short-term mitigation we can ask them to apply quickly.
Sam: Mitigations to suggest:
- Use a fresh AbortController per attempt (simplest)
- Prefer idempotent retries only for POSTs (don't retry by default)
- Upgrade to undici@5.x / Node 20's fetch where possible
- If behind a corporate proxy, test with direct connection or a different proxy implementation
Also file a ticket to update our SDK docs about AbortController usage and proxy caveats.
support-bot: Created incident record INC-4123 and attached customer logs. https://redwood.atlassian.net/browse/INC-4123
Raj: I'm going to open a small SDK patch: add a runtime warn if a caller passes the same AbortSignal across retries and we're in retry mode. PR will propose detecting "signal" reuse in our retry helper and logging a developer-visible warning.
Matt: Add a unit test covering proxy-agent + signal abort handoff if possible. For the docs, add an example showing per-attempt controllers and a recommended retry policy example for POSTs.
Lina: Customer will test the per-attempt controller pattern this afternoon and report back. I'll update the ticket with their result.
Sam: TL;DR — root cause likely signal-propagation differences + proxy-agent behavior in older undici; reproducible locally; short-term fix = new AbortController per retry or avoid retrying POSTs. Long-term = SDK defensiveness + docs + possibly guard in retry helper.
Lina: Thanks — marking for eng-assist and keeping customer in the loop. :thumbsup:
